==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gmh|de}} {{term|houwen|lang=gmh}}, {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|houwan|lang=goh}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*hawwaną}}. Cognate with English {{term|hew|lang=en}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|[ˈhaʊ̯ən]|[ˈhaʊ̯n]|lang=de}}
* {{hyphenation|hau|en|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=7|haut|[[haute]]''' ''or archaic'' '''[[hieb]]|[[gehauen]]''' ''or colloquial'' '''[[gehaut]]}}

# {{context|transitive|colloquial|lang=de}} to [[beat]]; to [[hit]] (someone); to [[spank]]
#: ''Ich '''hau''' dich gleich.'' — “Now I’m going to spank you.”
# {{context|reflexive|colloquial|of multiple people|lang=de}} to [[fight]]; to [[tangle]]
# {{context|transitive|lang=de}} to [[chop]]; to chop down; to [[cut]]; to [[whittle]]; to [[hew]]

====Conjugation====
In casual speech, {{term|hauen}} is often conjugated like a weak verb, with the past tense {{term|haute}}, etc.

